# dropnode fact sheet

## domain

- a Node is a rectangular-bounded element on the Canvas carrying Text and an optional visual Shape, with a unique id, a position, and dimensions; its Text dictates a minimum size (GraphNode in code)
- Text is the formattable content of a Node (required) or Connection (optional): blocks of styled runs supporting bold, italic, highlight, link, font size S/M/L, and bulleted lists (ADR-0009)
- Formatting is applied via the Formatting Toolbar shown while editing Text; Groups carry a plain single-line Label instead of Text
- a Group is a distinct kind of node (kind 'group') that visually and logically contains child nodes via their parentId; nesting is one level deep — a Group never contains another Group
- a Connection is a directed curve linking a Handle on a source Node to a Handle on a target Node, identified by its four endpoint fields
- a Reroute Point is a user-placed intermediate point that a Connection passes through; it belongs to the Connection's geometry and is not an independent Graph State element
- a Handle is a connection anchor on one of the four edges of a Node (top, right, bottom, left); its position is derived from the Node's rect
- a Resize Grip is a draggable control at one of the four corners of the selected node, used to change the node's size; distinct from a Handle
- the Canvas is the unbounded workspace containing all Nodes and Connections
- the Viewport is the visible region of the Canvas, defined by a pan offset and a zoom scale
- the Graph State is the complete serializable model: the arrays of all Nodes, all Connections, and all Pins, and nothing else (ADR-0025)
- a Command is an undoable user action exposing execute(), undo(), and a description
- the History is the ordered record of executed Commands, split into an undo stack and a redo stack
- the Snap Zone is a 30-canvas-unit radius around a Handle; a dragged connection endpoint inside it attaches to that Handle
- an Alignment Guide is a transient red line shown while dragging a Node when one of its edges or center axes lines up with an edge or center of another visible Node; it is never part of Graph State
- Alignment Snap is the magnetic pull that locks a dragged Node onto a nearby Alignment Guide within a small threshold, distinct from the Snap Zone which attaches connection endpoints to a Handle
- a Node owns exactly four Handles, one per edge; a Group carries the same four Handles
- deleting a Node cascade-deletes every Connection referencing it as source or target
- all Graph State lives in GraphService signals; components mutate it only through the service, transient drags directly and undoable actions via Commands
- Import replaces the Graph State wholesale after validation; Export serializes it to JSON

## text

- label: Text is stored in a library-neutral wire format: blocks of kind 'paragraph' (runs) or 'bullets' (items of runs); a run carries text plus optional bold/italic/highlight (literally true), link (http(s) only), and size ('S' or 'L' — absent means M)
  command: grep -q "kind: 'paragraph'" src/app/models/text.ts
- label: the Text editor is ProseMirror with a schema locked to exactly the supported formats; a pure converter maps PM doc JSON to Text at the editor boundary and PM never leaks into Graph State (ADR-0010)
  command: grep -q 'prosemirror-model' src/app/components/text-editor/text-schema.ts && grep -q 'docToText' src/app/components/text-editor/text-doc.ts
- inside the Text editor Enter inserts a new line (or next bullet in a list); blur or Ctrl+Enter commits, Escape cancels
- while editing, Ctrl+Z/Ctrl+Y are ProseMirror-internal; a whole edit session commits as one SetNodeTextCommand or SetConnectionTextCommand and unchanged content pushes nothing onto History
- the Formatting Toolbar shows for the entire edit session, floating above the target (flipping below when clipped at the viewport top); toggle buttons reflect the selection state and clicking them never blurs the edit
- the S/M/L buttons light a size only when the entire selected text shares it — opening an edit shows a uniformly sized Text at its own size, a mixed-size selection lights no button, and a bare caret reads the size typing would continue at @implemented
- the Link control opens an inline URL input in the toolbar; entered URLs without a scheme are normalized to https://
- font sizes render as Node S/M/L = 11/14/18 px and Connection S/M/L = 10/12/15 px; M matches the pre-Text rendering exactly
- Highlight renders as a yellow marker behind the text; links render underlined and Ctrl+Click opens them in a new tab (noopener) — plain click keeps select/drag
- pasted rich content keeps what maps to the supported formats and silently strips everything else (schema-driven)
- display-mode Text renders via plain Angular templates from the structured JSON — no innerHTML, no sanitizer
- committing empty Text on a Node reverts to the previous Text; committing empty Text on a Connection removes it (deliberate asymmetry, mirroring the old label rules)

## nodes

- double-clicking empty Canvas creates a Node with Text "New Node" centered on the cursor, as a single undo step
- new Nodes default to 160x48; Text wraps at the Node's width, and after render the measured content raises the width floor (widest unbreakable line, 120px minimum) and height (48px minimum) but never shrinks — manual sizes are grow-only protected
- Node auto-sizing updates go directly to GraphService and never create a History entry
- a regular Node carries an optional Shape — rectangle by default, with pill, diamond, and ellipse as the only non-default values; Groups never carry Shape @implemented
- Shape is visual-only: the Node's rectangular bounding box remains authoritative for Handles, hit testing, Marquee, alignment, containment, bounds, and Export, while Text stays centered in the shape's safe area @implemented
- choosing a Shape from the toolbar's Node trigger or the Command Palette applies it only to regular Nodes in the Selection as one undoable bulk Command; selected Groups are ignored, the Shape section is disabled while no regular Node is selected, mixed Shapes show no active item, and an all-no-op choice creates no History entry @implemented
- a Shape change grows an undersized regular Node around its existing center using a shape-specific Text-safe minimum and never shrinks an existing rect; Resize Grips remain free width/height resizing subject to that minimum @implemented
- four Resize Grips show at the corners of a node only while it is the entire Selection (exactly one Node, nothing else); dragging one resizes with the opposite corner anchored
- a grip drag clamps width and height to the Text-derived minimums (floors 120 and 48)
- an entire grip drag is one undo step: per-mousemove size updates bypass History, and a single ResizeNodeCommand is pushed on mouseup only if the rect changed more than 2px
- double-clicking a Node opens the Text editor with the content focused and selected; blur or Ctrl+Enter commits, Escape cancels; empty or unchanged Text is never committed
- setting a Node's Text is undoable and restores the exact previous Text including formatting
- deleting an unknown Node id throws "Node {id} not found"; deleting an unknown Connection id is a silent no-op
- Node ids follow the pattern node_{timestamp}_{counter} and Connection ids conn_{timestamp}_{counter}; the counter is monotonic within a session

## groups

- a Group is created either from the empty-Canvas Context Menu ("Add group": a 320x200 Group labeled "New Group" centered on the right-click point) or the Command Palette ("Add Group": centered in the visible Canvas, opening the Label editor); each creation is a single undo step, and Groups use the node_ id pattern
- membership is containment, judged by the node's center point on mouseup: center inside a Group's bounds joins it, center outside releases it — each change is one undo step
- when overlapping Groups both contain the drop point, the topmost-rendered Group (later in the nodes array) claims the node
- dropping a node into a Group it is connected to succeeds and severs those Connections: move, membership change, and severed Connections form one compound undo step restoring original ids
- dragging a Group moves it and all its children rigidly as one undo step; children keep absolute Canvas coordinates (ADR-0005)
- dragging a child inside its Group only moves the child; membership is unchanged
- deleting a Group releases its children in place (un-parented, positions kept) and removes the Group's own Connections as one compound undo step; undo restores membership and Connections with original ids
- a Group cannot be connected to its own children in either direction: createConnection returns null, connection drags never snap to the counterpart, and Import rejects such payloads
- a Group can be resized no smaller than its children's bounding box plus 16 canvas units of padding; a childless Group clamps to the same 120x48 floor as a Node; resize never changes membership
- label: double-clicking a Group's label strip edits its label inline; double-clicking its body creates a "New Node" child at the cursor as one undo step
  command: grep -q 'createChild' src/app/components/node/node.ts
- Groups render beneath Connections and regular nodes (ADR-0008 stacking) so children stay visible; their fill is solid (no alpha)

## colors

- label: the palette holds exactly 8 curated colors (NODE_PALETTE); an absent color field means the default node background
  command: grep -q 'NODE_PALETTE' src/app/models/node.ts
- the Node styling trigger's Color section (default + 8 palette colors) appears in the toolbar while any Node is in the Selection; choosing a swatch recolors every selected Node as one compound undo step, skipping Nodes already carrying the color — an item reads active only when ALL selected Nodes share its value, and an all-no-op click touches nothing
- Import rejects color values outside the palette wholesale with "Invalid node {id}: color must be a palette color"

## connections

- dragging from a Handle rejects self-connections and exact duplicates silently (createConnection returns null); reverse-direction and different-handle pairs between the same two Nodes are allowed
- label: during a connection drag, a dragged endpoint snaps to the nearest Handle within a 30-canvas-unit Snap Zone; the snapped Handle highlights
  command: grep -q 'snapThreshold = 30' src/app/components/connection-layer/connection-layer.ts
- the source Node's own Handles are never snap targets during a connection drag
- label: Quick-add: dropping a connection drag in empty space (no snap) past the 2px movement threshold spawns a "New Node" (160x48) connected from the dragged Handle; at or under the threshold the drop creates nothing
  command: grep -q 'QuickAddNodeCommand' src/app/services/commands.ts
- a quick-added Node is anchored so its incoming Handle — always the opposite of the source Handle, matching the ghost bezier — sits exactly at the drop point; "empty space" means no snap target, even over another Node's body
- a quick-add drop point inside a Group's bounds parents the spawned Node into it (topmost-rendered Group wins), except when the source is that very Group: the Node spawns unparented and the Connection is still created
- quick-add spawn, parenting, and Connection are one undo step that also selects the spawned Node (redo re-selects); the auto-opened Text editor is UI-only — it follows the standard edit rules, commits as a separate SetNodeTextCommand, never reopens on redo, and Escape cancels only the edit
- during a connection drag a dashed ghost bezier renders from the source Handle to the cursor
- clicking a Connection path or its Text card selects the Connection; Delete or Backspace deletes it as one undoable Command; Ctrl+click toggles its Selection membership instead
- the Connection styling trigger (Color, Arrowhead, Stroke Pattern, and Stroke Weight sections) shows while any Connection is in the Selection and restyles every selected Connection as one compound undo step; a control reads active only when ALL selected Connections share its value
- the Stroke Pattern (solid/dashed/dotted) and Stroke Weight (thin/normal/thick) sections of the Connection styling trigger follow the same toolbar rules: shown while any Connection is in the Selection, one compound undo step over all selected Connections, active only on a shared effective value, and an all-no-op click touches nothing @implemented
- a Connection stores optional strokePattern and strokeWeight fields; absent means solid/normal, setting a field back to its default removes it, and createConnection writes neither (ADR-0020) @implemented
- Import rejects a payload wholesale when strokePattern is outside solid/dashed/dotted or strokeWeight is outside thin/normal/thick, and canonicalizes explicitly-default stroke values to absent @implemented
- dash and dot rhythms are computed as multiples of the stroke width so a pattern reads the same at every weight; Arrowheads always draw solid — they match the stroke's color and weight, never its pattern (ADR-0020) @implemented
- a selected or hovered Connection thickens by a fixed increment on top of its own Stroke Weight (hover +1px, selected +1.5px) and keeps its own-color glow; normal reproduces the pre-feature 2.5/3.5/4 widths exactly @implemented
- label: every Connection renders an invisible solid 12px companion path that is the sole pointer target, so dotted and thin Connections stay exactly as clickable as solid ones; PNG export strips it (ADR-0020)
  command: grep -q 'connection-hit' src/app/components/connection-layer/connection-layer.ts && grep -q 'connection-hit' src/app/services/export-image-renderer.ts
- a Connection carries optional Text; double-clicking its Text card opens the Text editor with the content focused and selected @implemented
- Connection Text editing commits on blur or Ctrl+Enter, Escape cancels, and committing empty removes the Text (deliberate deviation from the Node rule) @implemented
- setting or clearing Connection Text is one undoable SetConnectionTextCommand; committing unchanged content pushes nothing onto History; createConnection never sets Text
- Connection Text renders on an opaque wrapping card with a max-width of 240px @implemented
- Connection Text cards center on their stored position along the complete Connection route and move with that route @implemented
- a Connection Text card is part of the Connection's hit area @implemented
- a Connection stores an optional textPosition @implemented
- without Reroute Points, textPosition keeps the cubic Bézier parameter meaning; with Reroute Points, it means normalized arc-length progress along the composite curve (ADR-0013, ADR-0021) @implemented
- textPosition is clamped to [0.1, 0.9] and absent means the route midpoint @implemented
- textPosition may only exist alongside Text @implemented
- dragging a Connection Text card selects the Connection and arms the drag on mousedown @implemented
- Connection Text movement beyond 2px updates GraphService transiently and mouseup pushes one MoveConnectionTextCommand only when it moved @implemented
- while dragging Connection Text, a projected point within 15 canvas units of the curve midpoint snaps to it; a drag ending snapped stores no textPosition @implemented
- clearing Connection Text discards its textPosition; undoing the clear restores both text and position in one step; newly added Text always starts at the midpoint @implemented
- Import rejects a payload wholesale when textPosition is not a number, is outside [0.1, 0.9], or is present on a Connection without text; Export emits textPosition only when present @implemented
- Connections without Reroute Points render as the existing cubic Bézier @implemented
- a Connection with Reroute Points renders as a bounded piecewise cubic Bézier through its source Handle, every ordered Reroute Point, and its target Handle @implemented
- routed Connection endpoint tangents follow their Handles @implemented
- routed Connection endpoint tangent magnitudes use the plain curve's span-based offset (clamp(0.4 × span, 40, 150)), capped so the departure/arrival control point never passes the first/last Reroute Point along the Handle direction @implemented
- Handle positions are the midpoints of the Node's four edges, computed from x, y, width, height
- a Connection may carry 1–32 ordered Reroute Points as absolute Canvas coordinates `{x, y}` @implemented
- Reroute Point coordinates are finite @implemented
- absent Reroute Points mean no route, and an empty imported array canonicalizes to absent @implemented
- Import rejects malformed Reroute Point coordinates wholesale and leaves existing Graph State untouched @implemented
- Import rejects non-finite Reroute Point coordinates wholesale and leaves existing Graph State untouched @implemented
- Import rejects more than 32 Reroute Points wholesale and leaves existing Graph State untouched @implemented
- Import rejects consecutive duplicate Reroute Points wholesale and leaves existing Graph State untouched @implemented
- moving an endpoint Node leaves a Connection's absolute Reroute Points fixed @implemented
- Reroute Point order never reorders automatically @implemented
- Reroute Point self-intersection is allowed @implemented
- double-clicking a Connection curve projects onto the nearest composite curve position and inserts one Reroute Point in route order @implemented
- at a Connection self-intersection, the earliest route segment wins for projection @implemented
- double-clicking a visible Reroute Point removes it @implemented
- dragging a Reroute Point selects its parent Connection and uses the 2px threshold @implemented
- Reroute Point dragging updates transiently and commits one undoable Command only when the point moves @implemented
- Reroute Point markers appear only for a selected or actively dragged Connection @implemented
- Reroute Points are Connection subparts, never Selection members @implemented
- Ctrl+clicking a Reroute Point toggles the parent Connection without starting a drag @implemented
- right-clicking a Reroute Point opens the parent Connection's Context Menu @implemented
- Reroute Point markers are hidden in Present Mode @implemented
- Reroute Point markers are stripped from PNG Export @implemented
- Reroute Point dragging has no Alignment Snap or Handle Snap Zone @implemented
- Reroute Point dragging has no obstacle collision or automatic rerouting @implemented
- Tidy up re-anchors Reroute Points onto the new flow: each point is projected onto the straight corridor between the newly re-picked Handle midpoints keeping its signed perpendicular offset scaled by the span ratio and capped at 25% of the new span, first/last points are pushed along their Handle directions so the routed endpoint sweeps match the plain curve, points within 6 canvas units of the corridor drop, and a route with no shape left is removed (rendering the plain curve); re-anchored points round to whole canvas units, keeping tidy of a tidied graph a no-op @implemented
- Copy, Paste, Duplicate, and Alt+drag translate copied Reroute Points by the graph fragment's Canvas delta @implemented
- rendering, hit-testing, and Marquee selection use the complete routed curve @implemented
- Zoom to Fit, Zoom to Selection, Connection Text projection, and PNG bounds use the complete routed curve @implemented
- creating a Connection and Quick-add never invent Reroute Points @implemented

## alignment

- label: dragging a Node shows red Alignment Guides and snaps its edges and center axes onto other visible Nodes within a screen-space threshold of ~6px (ALIGNMENT_SNAP_THRESHOLD over zoom), a deliberate divergence from the canvas-unit Snap Zone (ADR-0017)
  command: grep -q 'ALIGNMENT_SNAP_THRESHOLD = 6' src/app/models/alignment.ts
- alignment matches all three reference axes per dimension (left/center/right on X, top/middle/bottom on Y) in a full 3x3 pairing; the X and Y axes snap independently and the nearest match wins on each @implemented
- a guide is drawn for every visible Node coincident at the snapped position, each line spanning the dragged Node plus those Nodes; candidates are Viewport-only and all guides vanish on mouseup @implemented
- a dragged Group aligns by its own rect and a multi-Selection by the union of its roots; the moving set — the dragged Nodes plus any dragged Group's children — is never an alignment candidate @implemented
- Alt+drag Duplicates align on the same drag path, with the stationary original a valid candidate @implemented
- a Resize Grip drag aligns and snaps only its two moving edges (the dragged corner's vertical and horizontal edge) against candidates' edges and centers — the anchored edges and the resized rect's centers never participate; the Text-derived minimums and a Group's child-padding clamp override the snap, and an overridden snap shows no guide @implemented
- Align (left/center/right/top/middle/bottom) and Distribute (horizontal/vertical) are Commands on the multi-Selection: each repositions the Selection's roots — loose Nodes and Groups as rigid units with their children, Connections following their endpoints — as one compound undo step, distinct from drag-time Alignment Snap @implemented
- Align moves each root along one axis to an edge or center-line of the roots' union bounding box, never touching sizes or the other coordinate; roots already in place add no undo weight and a fully aligned Selection pushes nothing onto History (the factory returns null) @implemented
- Distribute sorts the roots by center along its axis, anchors the two outermost, and equalizes the edge gaps between neighbors; center ties keep their existing order, and when the roots overflow the span the shared gap goes negative — overlap is accepted @implemented
- Align and Distribute never change Group membership: no root is adopted into a Group it lands on and no child is orphaned by being pushed outside its Group's frame — a child may be left overhanging (ADR-0018) @implemented
- the toolbar shows an Align trigger (Align and Distribute sections) while the Selection holds at least two node roots, with the two Distribute items disabled below three roots; the multi-Selection Context Menu carries the same eight actions in an "Align" submenu under the same rules @implemented

## tidy up

- label: the toolbar Tidy up button (beside the zoom cluster) recomputes every Node's position into a fixed left-to-right layered arrangement following Connection direction, always the whole graph, as one TidyUpCommand undo step (ADR-0019) @implemented
  command: grep -q 'buildTidyUpCommand' src/app/services/commands.ts
- label: the engine is a hand-rolled pure module — cycle-breaking, longest-path layering, barycenter ordering sweeps, coordinates from real Node rects — no dagre or elkjs (ADR-0019); spacing constants are 120 between layers, 40 within a layer, 80 between components, 40 loner-grid gutters @implemented
  command: sh -c "grep -q 'TIDY_LAYER_GAP = 120' src/app/models/tidy-layout.ts && ! grep -q 'dagre' package.json"
- Tidy up is two-level: each Group contracts to one unit at the top level (Connections touching a child are promoted to its Group), its children lay out inside from intra-group Connections only, and the Group's rect becomes exactly its children's bounds plus the 16-unit padding, with 28 extra units of headroom above the children so they never cover the label strip — childless Groups keep their size, and regular Node sizes are never touched @implemented
- Tidy up re-picks every Connection's sourceHandle and targetHandle to follow the flow: forward Connections face the counterpart along the dominant axis between the new rects (ties fall horizontal), a Connection pointing against the flow (a cycle's back edge) arcs over the row via top/top Handles, and a forward corridor that would hide behind another Node's card detours under via bottom/bottom — exact duplicates may still collapse onto identical endpoints, accepted as cosmetic @implemented
- disconnected components stack vertically largest-area-first; Connection-less regular Nodes gather into a grid after the last component; a Connection-less Group still stacks as a one-unit component, never in the grid @implemented
- the tidied arrangement anchors its bounding-box top-left at the old graph bounds' top-left; results are deterministic (every tie breaks on nodes array order) and idempotent — an empty or already-tidy graph builds no Command and pushes nothing onto History @implemented
- after a tidy executes, the toolbar runs the standard Zoom to fit (viewport-only, no History entry); the Selection is neither read nor cleared, and undo restores positions, Group rects, Handles, and Reroute Points exactly with ids untouched @implemented

## viewport

- zoom is clamped to the range 0.1 to 5
- wheel zoom steps by 0.1 per tick, centered on the cursor so the point under the cursor stays fixed on screen
- toolbar zoom buttons and the Zoom In / Zoom Out Palette Entries step by 0.1 centered on the visible Canvas' center, so the Canvas center point stays fixed on screen — the same centering rule as wheel zoom with a different anchor
- left-drag on empty Canvas draws a Marquee, not a pan (ADR-0016); panning is hold-Space + left-drag (grab cursor, from anywhere, even over elements) or middle-mouse-drag, and never changes the Selection
- touch and pen input add two gestures the mouse lacks: one finger on empty Canvas pans (touch has no Space or middle button) and two fingers pinch-zoom around their midpoint; the Canvas subtree is touch-action: none, so Node/Handle/Text-card/Pin drags stream compatibility mouse events instead of being canceled mid-drag, and the touch pan's compatibility mousedown never arms a Marquee @implemented
- a Canvas with no stored Viewport to restore (fresh Scratch Canvas, corrupt-Project fallback) initializes at pan (0,0) and zoom 1; importing or clearing a graph does NOT reset the Viewport
- every page load opens framed: whichever graph activation lands — a stored Project, a ?data share link, or the stashed Scratch snapshot — applies Zoom to Fit instead of restoring its remembered Viewport, and switching Projects on the same route re-frames; the deferred fit waits one frame for the laid-out canvas size, and an empty graph keeps the default Viewport @implemented
- pan and zoom apply as one shared CSS translate+scale transform covering both the DOM node layer and the SVG connection layer
- Zoom to fit frames the whole graph — all Nodes (Groups included) unioned with every Connection's curve bounds so a bowing curve is never clipped — into 90% of the visible Canvas, centered on both axes, never magnifying past 1x; an empty graph is a silent no-op @spec
- Zoom to selection frames the selected element — a Node by its rect, a Group unioned with its children, a Connection by its cubic bezier bounds (the control-point box, which encloses the bow) — into 90% of the Viewport, centered, magnifying no further than 2x; no selection is a silent no-op @spec
- Zoom to fit and Zoom to selection are bounds-centered — a zoom-centering behavior distinct from the anchored zooms (cursor-centered wheel zoom, Canvas-center-centered toolbar buttons and Palette Entries); both write pan+zoom instantly within the [0.1,5] clamp, add no History entry, and persist via the normal Viewport auto-save @spec
- the toolbar zoom cluster carries a Zoom to fit button; Zoom to selection is keyboard-only, with no toolbar button or Context Menu entry @spec

## minimap

- the Minimap is a small canvas-rendered map of the whole graph overlaid at the editor's bottom-right, diverging from the main Canvas's DOM/SVG rendering so per-frame redraws stay inside the ADR-0003 budget (ADR-0024)
- the Minimap frames the fit-to-content bounds — the union bounding box of all Nodes and Groups padded by 10% per side, contain-fitted and centered in its 200x150 box
- the Minimap draws Connections as hairline curves along each route's segments, Groups as lighter rects, regular Nodes as filled rects, the Selection in an accent color, and the current Viewport as an outlined rectangle on top; Reroute Point handles, Connection Text, Shapes, and Palette colors are not drawn
- pressing or dragging anywhere on the Minimap recenters the Viewport on that world point; recentering is a Viewport operation that keeps the current zoom and adds no History entry
- the Minimap is hidden while the graph is empty, during Present Mode, and when toggled off; the toggle is the "Toggle Minimap" Palette Entry and persists under the localStorage key dropnode:minimap-hidden (visible by default)

## present

- Present Mode is entered from a toolbar Present button in the right cluster, disabled with a "Group nodes to present them" hint while the graph has no Groups; it works on the Scratch Canvas and Project routes alike and has no entry shortcut (ADR-0020) @implemented
- every Group is a Step, childless ones included; loose Nodes are never framed; Steps run in reading order of the Group's top-left corner — y first, x tiebreak, nodes-array order beyond that — and reordering means moving Groups (no stored order) @implemented
- entering Present Mode clears the Selection, snapshots the current Viewport, and animates to Step 1; each Step frames its Group unioned with its children into 90% of the visible Canvas, centered, magnifying no further than 2x — the Zoom-to-selection contract, with no bezier union for internal Connections @implemented
- Step transitions animate pan and zoom together over 500ms ease-in-out via rAF writes to the normal Viewport signal; a keypress mid-flight retargets from the in-flight position, and prefers-reduced-motion turns every transition into an instant jump to the same destination @implemented
- ArrowRight/ArrowDown/Space/Enter/PageDown advance and ArrowLeft/ArrowUp/PageUp go back (auto-repeat ignored); both ends are hard no-ops and Escape is the sole exit, restoring the exact pre-Present Viewport instantly @implemented
- Present Mode hides the Sidebar and toolbar, suppresses toasts, and shows a single non-interactive "current / total" Step counter as the only overlay; the browser Fullscreen API is never used @implemented
- while presenting the graph is read-only: mutation, Selection building, the Context Menu, and all global shortcuts are dead, and Handles and hover affordances disappear; free-roam wheel zoom and Space/middle pan stay live, as does Ctrl+Click on Text links — the next keypress re-frames from wherever the Viewport wandered @implemented
- Viewport auto-save is suspended for the whole Present session — the pre-Present Viewport is what stays persisted, and a mid-Present reload lands there @implemented

## selection

- the Selection is a set freely mixing Nodes and Connections — empty, one, or many (ADR-0015); it is transient interaction state, never serialized into Graph State, exports, or share links
- a Group is selected as a unit: its children ride along implicitly and are never Selection members beside their own Group (the set normalizes them away); a child is individually selectable only while its Group is not selected
- a Marquee (empty-Canvas left-drag past the 2px threshold) selects by touch: a Node by rect overlap, a Group via its own or any child's rect, a Connection when the rect touches its sampled curve or its Text card; touched elements highlight live and the Selection commits on mouseup
- a plain Marquee replaces the Selection; Ctrl+Marquee adds the touched elements to the pre-drag Selection; a sub-2px press-release is a click — plain clears the Selection, with Ctrl it is a no-op
- plain-click on an unselected element collapses the Selection to it; Ctrl+click toggles membership; mousedown on a member of a multi-Selection keeps the set and arms a drag — releasing without a drag collapses to that element (deferred collapse)
- Ctrl+A selects every Group (as a unit), every loose Node, and every Connection
- dragging any member moves the whole Selection rigidly — every root shifts by the same delta, Connections follow their endpoints — as one compound undo step; Group membership is frozen during a multi-Selection drag (single-element drags keep the center-point join-on-drop rule)
- Delete/Backspace on a multi-Selection removes everything as one compound undo step: explicitly selected Connections, each Node with its cascaded Connections, and a selected Group together WITH its children (deliberate divergence — a single-Group Delete still releases them); undo restores all original ids
- a Selection of exactly one element behaves exactly as single-select always did — grips, editors, menus, and membership rules are unchanged

## history

- Ctrl+Z undoes and Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes; there is no Ctrl+Y binding
- executing or pushing any new Command clears the redo stack
- undo and redo on empty stacks are silent no-ops; the stacks have no size limit
- an entire Node drag is one undo step: per-mousemove position updates bypass History, and a single Command is pushed on mouseup only if movement exceeded 2px in either axis and the net position actually changed (Alignment Snap can pull a small nudge back to the origin, pushing nothing) — a MoveNodeCommand normally, a compound when the drop also changed Group membership, a MoveGroupCommand for Group drags
- a plain click on a Node selects it without creating a History entry
- deleting a Node via Delete/Backspace removes the Node and its Connections as one compound undo step; undo restores the Node and its Connections with their original ids
- Import does not clear History, so undoing after an Import can reference stale ids
- switching between Projects (or to the Scratch Canvas) clears History — undo never crosses a Project boundary

## import-export

- Import is validate-then-replace: an invalid payload is rejected wholesale with a specific error message and existing state stays untouched
- Import validation rejects duplicate Node ids, Connections referencing Nodes absent from the same payload, and handle values outside top/right/bottom/left; an empty graph is valid
- Import validation also rejects kind values other than 'group', off-palette colors, parentId values that reference a missing node or a non-Group, Groups carrying a parentId, and Connections linking a Group to its own child
- Import accepts only rectangle, pill, diamond, or ellipse Shape values on regular Nodes, rejects Shape on Groups, and canonicalizes explicit rectangle to an absent field @implemented
- payloads without kind, parentId, or color import unchanged as plain nodes — pre-Group exports and links stay valid
- Import validation does NOT reject duplicate Connection ids or self-connections — only createConnection guards those
- Import whitelists Text wholesale: unknown block kinds or run keys, non-true format flags, sizes outside S/L, and non-http(s) links are rejected with "Invalid node {id}: {reason}" or "Invalid connection {id}: {reason}"; a Group carrying text is rejected, and a Group without a string label is rejected
- legacy payloads with plain-string labels migrate on import: a regular node's or connection's label becomes single-run Text (empty connection labels drop the field) — old exports and ?data links stay valid; Export always writes the new text shape
- label: the Export menu offers four items — Export as… (opens the export dialog), Copy JSON, Copy link, and a disabled Export to Drive placeholder hinting "Sign in required — coming soon"; it opens from the Scratch Canvas toolbar and from each project row's Export submenu in the Sidebar
  command: grep -q 'Export to Drive' src/app/components/toolbar/toolbar.ts && grep -q 'Export to Drive' src/app/components/sidebar/sidebar.ts && grep -q 'Export as' src/app/components/toolbar/toolbar.ts
- label: the "Export as…" dialog offers PNG and JSON formats with a live preview; it defaults to PNG + dark Export Theme on every open and persists nothing
  command: grep -q "format.set('png')" src/app/components/export-dialog/export-dialog.ts && grep -q "theme.set('dark')" src/app/components/export-dialog/export-dialog.ts
- the "Export as…" dialog closes on Escape, backdrop click, or its close button — dismissal has no side effects and downloads nothing
- the Export Theme (dark | light) applies only while rendering a PNG: it sets the image background and flips the dark-only defaults (Group chrome and Connection Text chips); default Node fills, Node Text, and the Connection stroke keep their on-screen appearance, applied Palette colors pass through, and nothing is ever written to Graph State or the JSON payload
- PNG export snapshots the on-screen graph via a foreignObject-serialized clone of the canvas-transform layer (ADR-0014); it is offered only from the Scratch Canvas toolbar and the currently open Project's Sidebar row — other project rows keep Export JSON file
- the PNG snapshot carries the body's computed text defaults (font stack, size, line height) onto its root, so exported Text renders in the same font as the editor instead of the SVG serif fallback
- label: the PNG captures the full graph bounds (all Nodes and every Connection's curve, so a bowing curve is never cropped) plus 40 canvas-unit padding at 2x scale, independent of pan/zoom, stripping Handles, Resize Grips, and selection chrome; an empty graph exports just the padded background
  command: sh -c "grep -q 'EXPORT_PADDING = 40' src/app/models/export-image.ts && grep -q 'EXPORT_SCALE = 2' src/app/models/export-image.ts"
- label: PNG is download-only (clipboard, link, and Drive stay JSON-only), named dropnode-graph.png on the Scratch Canvas or after the Project (slugged, fallback project.png), with a success toast; a failed render shows "Failed to export PNG" and downloads nothing
  command: grep -q "'.png'" src/app/services/export.service.ts
- label: a scoped PNG Export renders only its frozen root Nodes and Groups, each included Group's current children, and Connections whose two endpoints are inside that set; missing roots are skipped and a vanished scope renders the padded empty background @implemented
- scoped PNG Export uses the same 40 canvas-unit padding, 2x scale, Export Theme rules, and selection-chrome stripping as whole-graph PNG; it hides out-of-scope Nodes, Group chrome, Connection curves, and Connection Text @implemented
- a scoped PNG Export with exactly one labeled Group uses that Label's slug as the filename; a Node, multi-Selection, unlabeled Group, or other scope uses dropnode-selection.png @implemented
- the PNG preview runs the same renderPng pipeline as the download, regenerating debounced when the Export Theme changes; the JSON preview shows the exact pretty-printed payload
- label: Export downloads pretty-printed 2-space JSON named dropnode-graph.json with a success toast; Copy JSON copies the same JSON to the clipboard with a success/error toast
  command: sh -c "grep -q \"'dropnode-graph'\" src/app/services/export.service.ts && grep -q \"'.json'\" src/app/services/export.service.ts"
- a project row's Export actions read the stored graph from localStorage and never navigate; the file is named after the Project (slugged), falling back to project.json — except the open Project's row, whose "Export as…" dialog exports the live on-screen graph (matching its preview) under the same Project filename
- Copy link copies <origin>/?data=gz:<base64url of the gzip-compressed compact Graph State JSON>, dropping any existing path and query parameters — share links always land on the Scratch Canvas; the link is copied whatever its length, with a success/error toast (ADR-0026)
- share-link payloads are gzip+base64url under a gz: prefix because percent-encoded pretty JSON grew URLs past server request-line limits (browsers report HTTP 431); the payload decompresses via DecompressionStream at startup, so the URL load resolves asynchronously; an unprefixed raw-JSON ?data value from an older link still imports unchanged
- the Export menu is built on @angular/cdk/menu primitives; export side effects live in ExportService, never in the toolbar component
- exported Graph State is a copy; mutating it does not affect editor state
- label: on startup the app imports a Graph State from the URL query parameter named data; ?data is honored only on the Scratch Canvas route (/), never on /p/:projectId; a missing parameter is a silent no-op
  command: grep -q "params.get('data')" src/app/services/graph.service.ts
- invalid URL data shows an error toast prefixed "URL data invalid: "; a successful URL load toasts "Graph loaded from URL"
- the import dialog offers File Upload and Paste JSON tabs; file content takes precedence over pasted text, errors show inline and keep the dialog open

## collections

- a Collection is a named container of zero or more Projects shown as a parent entry in the Sidebar; Collections are flat — never nested
- every Project belongs to exactly one Collection; a Project is a saved graph with its own route /p/:projectId
- label: Collections and Projects — including each Project's Graph State and Viewport — persist in localStorage under the single schema-versioned key dropnode:collections (ADR-0007)
  command: grep -q "dropnode:collections" src/app/services/collection.service.ts
- Collection ids follow col_{timestamp}_{counter} and Project ids proj_{timestamp}_{counter}; duplicate Collection and Project names are allowed
- / redirects to the most recently opened Project; a ?data share link or an empty store stays on the Scratch Canvas
- an unknown or deleted /p/:projectId redirects to / with a "Project not found" error toast
- editing auto-saves: Graph State and Viewport changes persist to the current Project debounced at 300ms; there is no save button, and a bottom-left "Saving…/Saved" indicator reports the debounced write on Project routes only (the Scratch Canvas is never persisted and shows no indicator) @implemented
- the per-Project Viewport is stored outside Graph State — exported JSON and ?data links carry the graph's nodes, connections, and Pins, never the Viewport
- deleting a Collection cascade-deletes all its Projects; Collection and Project deletion are permanent (not undoable) and both guarded by a confirmation modal, the Collection modal stating its project count
- deleting the Project being viewed navigates to the most recently opened surviving Project, or to / when none remain
- Sidebar inline rename follows the node-label contract: Enter or blur commits, Escape cancels, empty or unchanged names are never committed; newly created Collections and Projects drop straight into rename
- "Import collection" (beside "New collection" in the Sidebar header) appends a brand-new Collection from the envelope file after validating every project graph wholesale — one bad project rejects the file; collection and project ids are regenerated so importing twice yields two independent copies
- collection export downloads <collection-slug>.dropnode-collection.json containing { name, projects: [{ name, graph }] } where graph is exactly the Graph State export shape
- Import on a project row navigates to that Project first, then opens the import dialog — a replace always happens on-screen
- the toolbar shows Import, Export, and Save-as-project only on the Scratch Canvas route; on /p/:projectId those actions live on the Sidebar row menus
- "Save as project" lists Collections in a dropdown and is disabled with a "Create a collection first" hint while no Collection exists
- the Scratch Canvas survives in-session navigation in memory only — a reload still clears it; "Save as project" consumes it
- label: per-Collection expand/collapse state persists under dropnode:sidebar-collapsed-collections
  command: grep -q "dropnode:sidebar-collapsed-collections" src/app/services/sidebar.service.ts

## shortcuts

- all keyboard shortcuts are ignored while focus is in an input, textarea, or contenteditable element
- Shift+F10 (or the Context Menu key) opens the Canvas context menu for the focused Node card, the focused Connection or Reroute Point, the selected Node/Connection, or the empty Canvas — the menu resolves its target exactly like a right-click, through the same synthetic-contextmenu pipeline @implemented
- every Node card is keyboard-focusable (never in Present Mode) and screen-reader named from its label or Text; Enter selects it, arrow keys nudge it 10px (1px with Shift) as undoable Move Commands — moving a member of a multi-Selection shifts the whole set as one compound undo step @implemented
- every Handle is keyboard-focusable (never in Present Mode) and labeled "Connect from <node name>"; Enter on a Handle arms a pending Connection (the keyboard twin of the drag), Tab cycles between Handles with the ghost following focus, Enter on the target Handle commits the same undoable CreateConnectionCommand as the drag, and Escape cancels — self-links, Group↔child, and duplicates reject with a reason toast @implemented
- every Connection is keyboard-focusable (never in Present Mode), screen-reader named "Connection from A to B", and selected by Enter (Shift+Enter toggles membership); [ and ] cycle the focusable Connections in document order (Shift extends Selection), dead while a pending Connection is composed @implemented
- the "Connect Nodes…" Palette Entry opens a dialog picking From/To Nodes with auto-facing Handles and the same validation as the drag path; it is unavailable until two Nodes exist @implemented
- Delete or Backspace deletes the Selection — a single Connection or Node exactly as before, a multi-Selection as one compound step; a focused Reroute Point is removed instead (refocusing its Connection); Escape clears the whole Selection and blurs the active element, except in Resize mode where it exits the mode keeping the Selection @implemented
- in Resize mode (or with Ctrl+arrows) arrow keys on a focused Node card resize it 10px (1px with Shift), the pressed-side edge moving with the opposite edge anchored and clamped to the shape/text minimum — one undoable ResizeNodeCommand per step; Resize mode toggles from the Node Context Menu and a Palette Entry and is exited with Escape @implemented
- a Connection's Reroute Points are keyboard-focusable (never in Present Mode); "Add Reroute Point" — from the Connection Context Menu or the Command Palette with a single Connection selected — appends one at the route midpoint and focuses it, Tab cycles the visible points, arrows move the focused point 10px (1px with Shift) as one undoable Command per step @implemented
- every Pin is keyboard-focusable (never in Present Mode) and labeled "Pin: <message>"; arrows shift a focused Pin 10px (1px with Shift) within its own anchor kind as one undoable MovePinCommand per step, and Enter opens its edit popover @implemented
- with nothing focused (focus on the page body or the Canvas containers), arrow keys pan the Viewport 40 screen px (10 with Shift), converted to world units by the current zoom — a Viewport change with no History entry, matching every other pan; the focus state owns the arrow grammar, so a focused Node card nudges/resizes and interactive elements keep native arrow behavior @implemented
- Ctrl+X cuts and Ctrl+C copies the Selection's Nodes and Groups; Ctrl+V pastes at the cursor; Ctrl+D duplicates the Selection's Nodes and Groups; Ctrl+A selects all — no selection (or an empty Clipboard for paste) is a silent no-op
- Shift+1 runs Zoom to fit and Shift+2 Zoom to selection, keyed off event.code (Digit1/Digit2) for layout independence and suppressed by the input-focus guard @spec

## clipboard

- the Clipboard is in-memory, single-entry, and session-scoped (ADR-0011): it holds the most recently Cut or Copied Selection and is lost on reload; the OS clipboard is never used for graph elements
- Cut, Copy, and Duplicate apply to Nodes and Groups only — a Selection with no Node offers them as silent no-ops
- copying a Selection captures its Nodes, each selected Group's children, and only Connections with BOTH endpoints inside the captured set — a selected Connection with an endpoint outside is silently dropped from the copy; the snapshot is a deep clone isolated from later edits
- Copy, Paste, Duplicate, project persistence, Graph State Export, and PNG rendering preserve a regular Node's Shape @implemented
- Copy keeps the original selected and never touches History
- Cut removes the captured set plus every touching Connection — and any explicitly selected dangling Connection, though it is never copied — as one compound undo step; unlike Delete, cutting a Group removes its children instead of releasing them; undo restores original ids while the Clipboard keeps the copy
- Paste regenerates all ids in the node_/conn_ patterns and remaps parentId and Connection endpoints; pasting the same entry twice yields two independent copies; a multi-element paste recenters the set's bounding box on the paste point
- menu Paste centers the copy on the right-click point; Paste on a Group's menu parents pasted regular nodes into that Group; a pasted Group never nests; Canvas paste strips parentId
- the Paste menu item renders disabled while the Clipboard is empty (the menu shape stays stable)
- Ctrl+V pastes centered on the tracked cursor position; repeated pastes without moving the mouse cascade by +24,+24 and moving the cursor resets the cascade
- Duplicate (menu item or Ctrl+D) copies the Selection's Nodes and Groups at +24,+24, each root keeping its original parentId (a sibling), as one undo step, without reading or writing the Clipboard
- holding Alt at drag start spawns a duplicate of the whole dragged Selection that the drag moves — the originals never move and mid-drag Alt changes are ignored; a single spawned node takes membership by the normal containment rule while a spawned set keeps membership frozen, the whole gesture is one undo step, and without >2px movement the spawn is aborted (restoring the source Selection)
- after Paste, Duplicate, or an Alt+drag drop the new copies become the Selection (inserted Group children stay implicit); Cut leaves nothing selected

## context menu

- right-clicking opens a Context Menu scoped to its target: empty Canvas offers Add node, Add group, and Paste; a Node offers Edit text, Cut, Copy, Duplicate, and Delete; a Group offers Add node, Rename, Cut, Copy, Paste, Duplicate, and Delete; a Connection offers Edit text, Add Reroute Point, and Delete; a member of a multi-Selection offers Cut, Copy, Duplicate, an Align submenu (shown with at least two node roots), and Delete acting on the whole set
- label: the Context Menu is built on @angular/cdk/menu CdkContextMenuTrigger and reuses the styled Helm dropdown-menu content; its logic lives in ContextMenuService, never in components
  command: grep -q 'CdkContextMenuTrigger' src/app/components/canvas/canvas.ts && grep -q 'ContextMenuService' src/app/services/context-menu.service.ts
- right-clicking a target that is part of a multi-Selection keeps the whole Selection and opens the multi menu; any other target collapses the Selection to itself before the menu opens; right-clicking empty Canvas clears the Selection and never starts a pan — panning is Space+left-drag or middle-mouse only
- label: Node, Group, and multi-Selection Context Menus offer Export as PNG above Delete; a lone Connection and empty Canvas do not, and the action freezes the surviving Selection roots before opening the scoped Export dialog @implemented
- Add node and Add group create centered on the right-click point (Node offset 60/24, Group offset 160/100); Add node on a Group creates a child of that Group at the point
- menu Edit text opens the target Node's or Connection's Text editor; menu Rename opens a Group's inline Label editor — all reuse the double-click editors and their commit rules
- menu Delete runs the same Commands as the Delete/Backspace shortcut, with identical cascade and undo behavior; opening or dismissing a menu never touches History
- the native browser context menu is suppressed on the Canvas, Nodes, Groups, and Connections, but preserved inside the Text editor and Group Label input (both stop propagation of the contextmenu event)

## pins

- label: a Pin is a single-user marker anchored to a Canvas point (absolute {x,y}) or a Node (nodeId plus an offset from the node's top-left), holding one non-empty plain-string message — never Text, never a Label; Pins are a third Graph State collection and serialize everywhere the document travels (ADR-0025)
  command: grep -q "kind: 'canvas'" src/app/models/pin.ts
- Pin ids follow the pin_{timestamp}_{counter} pattern @implemented
- a Node-anchored Pin moves with its Node (drags, Group moves, Tidy up); a Canvas-anchored Pin moves with nothing @implemented
- deleting a Node cascade-deletes its anchored Pins in the same compound undo step, and undo restores original ids; Groups are anchorable, and deleting a Group kills the Group's own Pins while its released children and their Pins survive @implemented
- Pin operations are full Commands: create-with-message, edit, move, and delete are all undoable @implemented
- creation is ghost-pin: "Add pin" (empty-Canvas, Node, and Group Context Menus, or a Palette Entry that anchors to the single selected Node at its top-right corner when exactly one is selected, else drops canvas-anchored at the Viewport center) opens the popover; nothing enters Graph State until a non-empty commit creates the Pin with its message as one Command; Escape or an empty commit leaves no trace and no History entry @implemented
- editing mirrors the Text editor contract on a plain textarea: click opens the popover focused and selected, Enter inserts a newline, blur or Ctrl+Enter commits, Escape cancels; an unchanged commit pushes nothing; committing empty removes the Pin; the Formatting Toolbar never appears @implemented
- dragging a Pin moves its anchor within its own kind — no node-to-canvas re-anchoring — with transient updates and one Command on mouseup past the 2px threshold @implemented
- Cut, Copy, Duplicate, and Alt+drag carry Pins anchored to captured Nodes; Paste regenerates Pin ids and remaps anchors; Canvas-anchored Pins never ride @implemented
- Import validates pins wholesale: rejects unknown anchor kinds, anchors referencing nodes absent from the same payload, non-finite coordinates or offsets, non-string or empty/whitespace messages, and duplicate Pin ids; a payload without a pins key imports pin-less and an empty array canonicalizes to absent; Groups are valid anchors; there are no count or message-length caps @implemented
- label: Pin visibility is a global toggle — the "Toggle Pins" Palette Entry, persisted under dropnode:pins-hidden, visible by default; hidden Pins still exist, still serialize, still ride the Clipboard, and still render in a PNG whose "Include Pins" checkbox is checked
  command: grep -q 'dropnode:pins-hidden' src/app/services/pin-visibility.service.ts
- PNG Export omits Pins unless the "Export as…" dialog's "Include Pins" checkbox is checked — default off, reset on every open, PNG-only; when included, capture bounds union the rendered Pin positions, and a scoped export renders only Pins anchored to in-scope Nodes (Canvas Pins never render scoped) @implemented
- Pins never join the Selection, are ignored by Marquee and Ctrl+A, never extend Zoom-to-fit bounds, are not drawn on the Minimap, and are hidden in Present Mode @implemented
- right-clicking a Pin offers "Edit pin" and "Delete pin"; Delete pin runs the same removal Command as an empty commit, and the pin Context Menu never touches the Selection @implemented

## ui

- a single toast shows at bottom-right; a new toast replaces the current one and resets the 4-second auto-dismiss timer

## architecture

- Nodes are DOM Angular components and Connections an SVG overlay, per ADR-0001 hybrid rendering
- rendering stacks in three layers inside the shared transform per ADR-0008: Group cards, then the SVG connection layer, then regular node cards — Connections are clickable over Group rects but stay occluded under regular node cards
- label: the perf-critical components (canvas, node, connection-layer) use OnPush change detection per ADR-0003
  command: grep -q OnPush src/app/components/canvas/canvas.ts && grep -q OnPush src/app/components/node/node.ts && grep -q OnPush src/app/components/connection-layer/connection-layer.ts
- the root App and Toast components do not declare OnPush, deviating from the ADR-0003 mandate that all components use OnPush
- the performance target is 200 Nodes with ~100 Connections; beyond that requires virtualization or canvas rendering (ADR-0003)
- label: built on Angular 22 standalone components with signals
  command: grep -q '"@angular/core": "^22' package.json
- label: change detection runs with zone.js; the app is not zoneless
  command: grep -q '"zone.js"' package.json
- label: the unit test suite (Vitest + jsdom) passes
  command: npx vitest run --reporter=dot
- the SVG connection layer sizes itself to the node extent plus 1000, with a 5000-unit minimum
